import ProductionLayout from '../layouts/ProductionLayout.astro';
---
<ProductionLayout title="Archive — Skyhook Industries" description="Archived early Skyhook Industries concepts preserved for project history." noindex={true}>
  <section class="page-hero paper-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Archive / project history</p>
    <h1>Old ideas stay visible. Old assumptions do not stay active.</h1>
    <p>Skyhook Industries has changed substantially over time. The original site's people, sources and daring questions belong in the story, even where its specifications and schedules no longer describe the current engineering work.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="paper-section">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <p class="chapter-label">Archived concepts</p>
      <div>
        <h2>Preserve the trail.</h2>
        <article class="archive-item">
          <p class="status-label">ARCHIVED CONCEPT — NOT CURRENT ROADMAP</p>
          <h3>Comet 26P / Grigg–Skjellerup mission proposal</h3>
          <p>The early site imagined a Skyhook as a first step toward reaching Comet 26P. Its proposed sequence moved from surveying and landing to robotic work with local material, then to a large trajectory change. It was an exploratory vision, not a validated mission design.</p>
          <ol class="archive-sequence">
            <li><b>01 / BUILD</b><span>Develop a working tether before attempting the comet expedition.</span></li>
            <li><b>02 / SURVEY</b><span>Reach 26P, inspect it and identify a place for robotic surface operations.</span></li>
            <li><b>03 / USE LOCAL MATERIAL</b><span>Investigate whether cometary material could support a propulsion system.</span></li>
            <li><b>04 / CHANGE COURSE</b><span>Explore a substantial change to the comet's trajectory toward an Earth encounter.</span></li>
          </ol>
          <p class="archive-caveat">The original page named 2024 and 2025 events; those dates are past. No comet intercept, extraction system or trajectory modification is part of the current roadmap.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="archive-item">
          <p class="status-label">RETIRED INHERITED VALUES</p>
          <h3>Legacy Skyhook specification set</h3>
          <p>The former website treated values such as exactly 4,252 km of tether, 64 interwoven Kevlar strands, roughly $1/kg material cost, a fixed hub altitude at one-third Earth radius, a 67 m “spider” grabber and calendar milestones through 2042 as if they were established design requirements. They are no longer active specifications.</p>
          <p>The distinctive questions remain: how a capture mechanism holds and releases a payload, how the structure survives damage, and how the system restores its orbit after each transfer. The <a href="/reference-architecture/">current study</a> keeps those questions open where evidence is still missing.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="paper-section archive-source-section">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <p class="chapter-label">Research lineage</p>
      <div class="archive-source-grid">
        <div>
          <h2>Keep the source in view.</h2>
          <p class="lede">The original Skyhook page led with Robert P. Hoyt and Chauncey Uphoff's cislunar tether paper. The paper remains part of the project's research lineage; its proposals are not proof of a finished Skyhook vehicle.</p>
          <a class="cta light" href="https://www.niac.usra.edu/files/studies/final_report/7Hoyt.pdf">Read the cislunar tether study ↗</a>
        </div>
        <aside class="archive-folio" aria-label="Cislunar Tether Transport System bibliographic record">
          <span>ARCHIVE / SOURCE 01</span>
          <strong>Cislunar Tether<br />Transport System</strong>
          <p>Robert P. Hoyt · Chauncey Uphoff<br />NASA Institute for Advanced Concepts · 1999</p>
          <small>Research source / not a current hardware specification</small>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="paper-section">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <p class="chapter-label">Current baseline</p>
      <div>
        <h2>Use the live system pages.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Current architecture, assumptions, research sources and program gates live on the main site and in the repository documentation.</p>
        <a class="cta light" href="/system/">Current system →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProductionLayout>
